System Design [Stream Processing]

Real Type Data Processing. Use Case:
1. Time based bucketing
2. CDC : Change Data Capture:
	DataBase Specific Events ====(data specific to database say writes/ transaction logs using WAL)===> BROKER =====> Derived Data
3. Event Sourcing: Clinet =====database-agnostic-events===> BROKER ====> Consumer =====> DataBase

Note:
What is a Broker: Black Box that passes messages from producers to consumers.
Broker Must ensure Exactly once message processing:
	1. To ensure atleast once : broker must be fault tolerant, persistant and replicated. Consumer must ack events.
	2. To ensure atmost once (Idempotency): Implemented via 2 phase commit(expensive) or Idempotent consumers using idempotency keys [Recomeneded]

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
											BROKERS: KAFKA and RabbitMQ
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. In Memory Message Brokers: Messages are stored temporarily (often memory-first) and delivered to consumers using load balancing.
	Eg: RabbitMQ and AmazonSQS

					  	   ___ConsumerA
	Q:	[4]-[3]-[2]-[1]===|___
							  ConsumerB
	Pros:
	Maximum throughput
	Cons:
	1. Messages can be out of order: say A consumes 1 and if B is free it can consume 2 and if job2 is small this may finish before 1.
		Load Balancing distributes messages across multiple consumers (or workers) to handle them in parallel.
		Redelivery (often due to temporary failures, timeouts, or consumer crashes) resends a message to any available consumer. 
	2. No fault tolerance or replayblity: Once acknowledged, messages are typically removed. If a consumer crashes after ack but before processing, data may be lost


2. Log Based Messgae Brokers: Messages are stored sequentially on disk and retained for a configurable duration.
	Eg: Kafka

						   ___ConsumerA
	Q:	[4]-[3]-[2]-[1]===|___
							  ConsumerB

	Pros:
		All consumers reading from a queue gets all message in order. (per partition)
		Messages are durable and can be replayed
	Cons: 
		A slow consumer can lag behind (head-of-line blocking per partition)
		Ordering is guaranteed only within a partition.

TLDR: Need ordering & durablity (say CDC or running Average)-> use Kafka.
	  Need simple task distribution (say newsfeed building, email or video-encoding) use SQS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
												STREAM ENRICHMENT (CDC)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use Case: Add additional context or attributes to events in a stream.

A. Stream  and Stream Joins

Stream A (id, val1) ─┐
                     ├─ Consumer (join on id) → Output Stream[id:val1+val2] (Sink Queue)
Stream B (id, val2) ─┘

Consumer caches events until the matching key arrives. Requires windowing / TTL to avoid unbounded state


B. Stream and Table Joins

Stream (id, val1) ─┐
                   ├─ Consumer (CDC-synced table cache) → Output Stream[id:val1+val2] (Sink Queue)
Table (id, val2) ──┘


Here: Consumer maintains a local cached copy of the table (CDC ensures consistency), and then join stream events as they arrive with table events to enrich them.

C. Table to Table Joins : CDC both table and Consumer maintains two local state stores and joins updates as they arrive.

CDC Scaling:
	1. Partition broker and consumers by join key.
	2. Consumers must be stateful, replicated, and fault tolerant. (Achieved by Stateful Stream Processing Frameworks)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
										STATEFUL STREAM PROCESSING (FLINK | SPARK STREAMING)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONSUMER PROPERTY: Each event must affect state exactly once, even during failures.

1. Micro-Batching (Spark Streaming)
	Data grouped into small time-based batches.
	State stored externally (e.g., Kafka, HDFS).
	On failure, only the failed batch is replayed

2. Checkpointing (Flink):
	Periodically snapshot: Nodes state, Offsets, Metadata.
	Snapshots stored in S3 / HDFS.
	On failure, job restarts from last checkpoint.

Implementation: Inject barriers messages into streams, Nodes on reciving barrier message checkpoint their state to S3.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
													CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CDC is superior to polling or DB joins for building derived data. Kafka + CDC enables scalable, replayable durable pipelines.
Stateful consumers are best implemented using Flink or Spark Streaming
